    "answer": "**Diagnosis: missing unicast forwarding flow for h5 on s2.**\n\nEvidence gathered:\n\n- `h1 \u2192 h5`: 100% ICMP loss.\n- `h2 \u2192 h5`: 100% ICMP loss.\n- `h1 \u2194 h2` works, so both hosts and their local access switch (`s4`) are functioning.\n- `h3 \u2192 h5` also fails. This rules out a fault unique to h1/h2 or the `s4\u2013s2` path.\n- `h6 \u2192 h5` works, confirming h5 and its attachment at `s6` are reachable locally.\n- `h7 \u2192 h5` works, confirming the `s3\u2013s6` path toward h5 works.\n- `h3 \u2192 h7` works, demonstrating that traffic can traverse `s5 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3` for other destinations.\n\nAll physical inter-switch links are reported ACTIVE by ONOS, so there is no observed link-down condition.\n\nThe key finding is in **s2\u2019s flow table**: it contains destination-MAC forwarding rules for h1, h2, h3, h4, h6, h7, and h8, but **no rule matching h5\u2019s MAC (`00:00:00:00:00:05`)**. There is also no catch-all unicast forwarding rule. Thus frames from h1/h2 (and h3) destined to h5 reach s2 and have no forwarding action there.\n\n**Fault location:** the forwarding/control-plane state on **s2**, specifically the absent flow for destination MAC `00:00:00:00:00:05` toward the upstream path (s2 port 1, toward s1). This is a flow-programming/state issue, not an observed physical-link or h5-host failure.",
